new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6ad82d2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,131 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy on one box
+
+This is the "one box" setup: a single Linux server with Docker that runs
+everything — web requests, background jobs, the SQLite databases and the
+uploaded files — in one container, with SSL handled by Kamal's proxy.
+
+Fork the repo, fill in your host name, your mail server and your secrets, run
+`kamal setup`, and you'll have Fizzy at `https://your-host` with data that
+survives the next deploy.
+
+Everything lives in [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml) and
+[`.kamal/secrets`](../.kamal/secrets).
+
+
+## What you need
+
+* **A server.** Any Linux box you can SSH into as root (or a user in the
+  `docker` group) with 2 GB of RAM or more. Kamal installs Docker for you if it
+  isn't there yet.
+* **A host name.** An `A`/`AAAA` DNS record pointing at the box, with ports 80
+  and 443 open. Let's Encrypt needs to reach port 80 to issue your certificate.
+* **A container registry.** Somewhere to push the image you build: Docker Hub,
+  GitHub Container Registry (`ghcr.io`), or your own.
+* **A mail server.** Fizzy signs people in by emailing them a magic link, so
+  outbound SMTP has to work. Any transactional provider or relay will do.
+* **Docker on your laptop**, to build the image, plus this repo checked out and
+  `bundle install` run, so that `bin/kamal` works.
+
+
+## 1. Fill in your details
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml` and replace the placeholders:
+
+| Placeholder             | Replace with                                            |
+| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `your-user/fizzy`       | Your image repository                                   |
+| `registry.server`       | Your registry (drop the line for Docker Hub)            |
+| `registry.username`     | Your registry user name                                 |
+| `fizzy.example.com`     | Your host name (in `servers`, `proxy.host` and `APP_HOST`) |
+| `SMTP_*`                | Your mail server address, port, user name and domain    |
+| `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`   | The address Fizzy sends from                            |
+
+Building for an ARM server (Ampere, Graviton, Hetzner CAX…)? Set
+`builder.arch: arm64`.
+
+
+## 2. Generate your secrets
+
+`.kamal/secrets` reads these from your environment, so export them in the shell
+you deploy from (or keep them in an untracked `.env` file you source, or have
+Kamal fetch them from a password manager — see the comments in the file):
+
+```sh
+export KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=...           # Registry password or access token
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(bin/rails secret)   # Signs cookies, keep it stable
+export SMTP_PASSWORD=...                     # Password for your mail server
+eval "$(bin/rails vapid:keys | sed 's/^/export /')" # Web push notification keys
+```
+
+Keep `SECRET_KEY_BASE` somewhere safe: changing it logs everyone out.
+
+
+## 3. Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+That installs Docker if needed, boots the proxy, pushes your image, requests a
+Let's Encrypt certificate and starts Fizzy. When it finishes, open
+`https://your-host` and sign up — the first account you create owns the
+install. The verification code is emailed to you, so if nothing arrives, check
+your `SMTP_*` settings with `bin/kamal logs`.
+
+Self-hosted Fizzy is single account by design: signups close once that first
+account exists. Set `MULTI_TENANT: true` in `config/deploy.yml` if you want to
+host more than one account on the box.
+
+Every deploy after that is just:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Database migrations run automatically when the container boots, and the old
+container keeps serving assets until the new one is healthy.
+
+
+## What runs where
+
+* **Web** — Thruster + Puma in the app container, behind `kamal-proxy` on ports
+  80/443. The proxy terminates SSL and forwards to the container, which is why
+  `RAILS_ENV=production` assumes SSL and redirects HTTP to HTTPS.
+* **Background jobs** — Solid Queue runs inside Puma on the same box
+  (`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: true`), so there's no separate jobs host to manage.
+  Recurring tasks from `config/recurring.yml` run there too.
+* **Databases** — SQLite: `production.sqlite3` plus separate databases for the
+  queue, cache and cable, all under `/rails/storage`.
+* **Files** — Uploads go to disk (`ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE=local`), also under
+  `/rails/storage`.
+
+
+## Data that survives deploys
+
+`/rails/storage` is a Docker volume (`fizzy_storage`), so your databases and
+uploads outlive containers, deploys and rollbacks. Deleting that volume deletes
+your Fizzy install, so back it up:
+
+```sh
+# Consistent snapshot of the main database inside the container...
+bin/kamal app exec --reuse "sqlite3 storage/production.sqlite3 '.backup storage/backup.sqlite3'"
+
+# ...then copy the whole volume off the box.
+ssh root@fizzy.example.com "tar -C /var/lib/docker/volumes/fizzy_storage/_data -cz ." > fizzy-backup.tar.gz
+```
+
+Uploads can be moved to S3-compatible storage instead by setting
+`ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE=s3` and the `S3_*` variables from
+`config/storage.oss.yml`.
+
+
+## Handy commands
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal console     # Rails console on the box
+bin/kamal dbconsole   # SQLite console
+bin/kamal shell       # A shell in the running container
+bin/kamal logs        # Tail the logs
+bin/kamal rollback    # Back to the previous version
+bin/kamal app exec "bin/rails db:migrate"
+```
